nt speeds so  of course he's never going to have been  in the same place at the exact same time  on either day however if you use  visualization to solve this problem then  the riddle becomes a lot more clear so  picture the monk walking up the mountain  and picture the monk walking down the  mountain now overlay those two images  and of course the two versions of the  monk must meet at some point on their  journeys they must cross paths as you  can see then they must have been at the  same point on that mountain at the same  time at some point during their journey  so you might have come to the right  conclusion using normal rational  thinking but as you can see you can make  the conscious choice to use  visualization as a form of thinking as a  form of problem-solving and sometimes  that's better suitably come up with the  correct answer more effectively than  other forms of thinking and this is  something that Einstein knew very well  that Einstein believed and we can see  this when we read about how he came up  with this theory of relativity and of  special relativity Einstein said that  when he came up with a theory of special  relativity he pictures himself falling  off of a roof and looked around to see  how that might look and when he came up  with relativity he pictures himself  traveling on a beam of light and then  visualized what the rest of the world of  the universe would look like to him  at that point it was so intuitive in his  visualization skills were so great he  was able to come up with the correct  answer and unlock the mysteries of the  universe just by picturing what his  brain intuitively knew would happen and  I've shown had some physical changes in  his brain that might have explained this  ability to he had larger regions that  were dedicated to visual spatial  reasoning and he also had a larger and  thicker corpus callosum which is a band  of neurons that crosses the left and  right hemispheres of the brain and  connects them so he would